Keys are underlined in red: CID, MID, PID and OID are single attribute primary keys. Product.MID and Order.CID are foreign keys shown with a small FK tag. OrderItems has the composite primary key (OID, PID) because an order line is identified by the order together with the product.
MANUFACTURER 1 --< PRODUCT with a crow's foot and a bar on the manufacturer side, so one manufacturer makes many products and each product has exactly one manufacturer.
I added the derived total price computed by a function and the trigger that checks the 2 kg weight. I added the total price and the weight check.
Design note: I chose CID, MID, PID and OID as single-attribute primary keys because each naturally identifies one customer, manufacturer, product or order. Product.MID and Order.CID are foreign keys for the two 1:N relationships. OrderItems needs the composite primary key (OID, PID) because an order line is only identified by the order together with the product, and one product may legitimately appear in many orders. The derived attribute ORDER_TOTAL is computed by fnOrderTotal(OID) and must not be stored, otherwise it can disagree with OrderItems. I added the index on OrderItems(OID) because the package weight trigger groups by OID on every delivery insert, and I validated the 2 kg limit in a trigger so the rule is enforced for every user of the database.
